# What gets EXCLUDED from the image build context.
#
# The first entry is the security-critical one. .env holds live OpenAI,
# Pinecone, LlamaCloud, Logfire, LangSmith and HeyGen keys, and anything
# copied into an image stays readable in that layer FOREVER -- deleting it in
# a later layer does not remove it, it only hides it from the final
# filesystem. Secrets reach the container at runtime instead, via
# `env_file: .env` in docker-compose.prod.yml.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Virtualenv: Windows binaries that cannot run on the Linux base image, and
# ~500MB of packages the Dockerfile reinstalls from requirements.txt anyway.
.venv/
venv/

# Private TLS keys for local/LAN HTTPS dev. Caddy issues its own certificates
# in production -- these must never leave this machine.
certs/

# Local working data. Ingestion runs on YOUR machine and pushes to Pinecone's
# cloud index; the server queries Pinecone directly and never reads these, so
# shipping the source PDFs and parsed Markdown would bloat the image for
# nothing.
data/
tmp/

# Test/eval caches and artifacts.
.pytest_cache/
.deepeval/
tests/

# Python bytecode -- regenerated inside the image, and .pyc files built on
# Windows are not reused on Linux anyway.
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/

# Docs. Not needed at runtime and they change constantly, which would
# needlessly invalidate the Docker build cache on every edit.
*.md

# Git and editor state.
.git/
.gitignore
.vscode/
.idea/

# The build/deploy files themselves -- read by Docker from outside the build
# context, never needed inside the image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.prod.yml
Caddyfile
